<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sambia Peninsula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samland Peninsula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sambia Peninsula, or Samland, lies on the Baltic coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, between the Curonian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> lagoons. Home of the Sambians, an Old Prussian people, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in the 1250s and divided with the Bishopric of Samland. It is the world's main source of Baltic amber.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambia-peninsula.jpg" alt="Sambia Peninsula" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Insider Andrein (Original) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaliningradsky_peninsula_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sambia is a peninsula bounded by the Curonian Lagoon to the north-east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a> to the south-west, the Pregolya River to the south and the Deyma River to the east. It is named after the Sambians, an extinct tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>. The landscape is hilly in the west, with coastal bluffs and beaches, and low and flat in the east; its highest point, Galtgarben, rises to 111 metres. Gvardeysk lies at its south-eastern end and Polessk at the north-eastern end, and the coast holds the port of Baltiysk and the seaside resorts of Zelenogradsk and Svetlogorsk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Amber has been found here for over two thousand years, and the ancient Amber Road led south from Old Prussian settlements such as Kaup in Sambia. In imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the right to collect it was restricted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern dynasty</a>, and from the 19th century it was mined on an industrial scale, by Germans before 1945 and by Soviets and Russians afterwards, at Yantarny. The Greek traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas</a> is thought to refer to the area as an amber island called Abalus.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> conquered Sambia in the 13th century and the Bishopric of Samland was established in 1243. Settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> arrived and the Sambians were gradually assimilated; the peninsula was the last area where Old Prussian was spoken, until the language died out at the beginning of the 18th century. After the Thirteen Years' War it was held from 1466 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Teutonic Order, and from 1525 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701 and part of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> in 1773.</p><p>It entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. In 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, including Sambia, and expelled its German inhabitants under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a>, was repopulated with Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a>, and until 1991 was largely a closed military area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sambia Peninsula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1243 the papal legate William of Modena divided Prussia into the bishoprics of Kulm, Pomesania, Ermland and <b>Samland</b>, each bishop ruling a third of his diocese as a territory; three of the cathedral chapters were incorporated into the Order, while Ermland kept more independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Founded in 1243 as one of William of Modena's four Prussian dioceses, <b>Samland</b> took shape after the conquest of the Sambians in 1255.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-samland">Bishopric of Samland</a></li></ul></section>
